   165	from the node-level nsys export of session 2 (`cuda_api_trace` +
   166	`cuda_gpu_trace`, capture window = the 5a latency block):
   167	`cudaGraphLaunch` calls/pass, `cudaLaunchKernel`-family calls/pass outside
   168	graph replay (eager launches), memcpy/memset API calls/pass, distinct kernel
   169	names inside vs outside replay; plus, for the Inductor arms, the
   170	`torch._inductor` cudagraph counters (`cudagraph_skips` and reasons,
   171	`TORCH_LOGS=cudagraphs`) and the cudagraph-trees recorded-graph count.
   172	Launch counts include runtime AND driver entry points (`cudaLaunchKernel`,
   173	`cudaLaunchKernelExC`, `cuLaunchKernel`, `cuLaunchKernelEx`; `cudaGraphLaunch`
   174	/ `cuGraphLaunch`). GPU events are attributed to graph identities via the
   175	node-level export's graph column (fallback: correlation with launch API
   176	rows, stated as a caveat). Fourfold classification per pass: (i) captured
   177	model computation (kernels inside replay); (ii) uncaptured model computation;
   178	(iii) external input staging (cudagraph-trees copies of non-static inputs
   179	into the static pool — `_foreach_copy_`/copy kernels/`cudaMemcpyAsync`);
   180	(iv) other uncaptured copies/memsets. Also reported: recorded-graph count
   181	(Inductor counters), graphs visited per pass, replay launches per pass, and
   182	compile/recording counter stability across settle → validation → timing.
   183	An arm is **fully graphed** only with positive replay evidence (≥1 graph
   184	launch per pass) AND zero uncaptured model kernels; multiple graphs per pass
   185	are legitimate; staging is timed overhead but not uncaptured model work.
   186	Arms verified: C1, C3, H (Inductor) and B, E, G (manual), from their
