   190	`fullmodel/boundary_bench.py`: chains of N **dependent** kernels, N ∈ {10,
   191	100, 1000, 2000}: (trivial) one Triton program loads ONE int32 element, adds
   192	1, stores it back to the same buffer — kernel k+1 depends on kernel k, and
   193	the final counter must equal the total launch count (exact-integer
   194	dependency proof); (elem37) an in-place value-preserving fp16 elementwise op
   195	on a 37 MB tensor, N ∈ {10, 100}; (gemm) alternating dependent GEMMs
   196	660×384×1536 / 660×1536×384 fp16 into preallocated ping-pong outputs with
   197	1/√K-scaled weights (bounded values), N ∈ {10, 100}. Modes: stream launches;
   198	one CUDA graph of the chain (replayed); programmatic dependent launch for the
   199	trivial chain (Triton 3.4 `launch_pdl=True`; `gdc_wait()` before the
   200	dependent load, `gdc_launch_dependents()` after the store; the PTX is checked
   201	for `griddepcontrol`), on a stream and inside a graph; real-kernel PDL is
   202	UNSUPPORTED through cuBLAS/ATen launches and recorded as such. Note: the
   203	triton 3.4.0 wheel's `language/extra/cuda/gdc.py` carries the pre-3.4
   204	`_builder=` signature and fails to compile under the 3.4 code generator
   205	(`_semantic=`); the bench therefore defines the same two one-line PTX
   206	intrinsics with the 3.4 signature (identical asm strings) — recorded here
   207	so the deviation from the library call is explicit. The elem37 chain's
   208	working set (37 MB) fits the RTX 5090's 96 MiB L2, so that chain measures
   209	the L2-resident rate, which is reported as the plan-1.4 L2 bandwidth input. Timing:
   210	unprofiled device time via CUDA events around the chain, median of 20
   211	repeats with min/max → `T(N)` per (chain, mode), plus least-squares slope
   212	and intercept over N. **`T(N)/N` and the slope are effective per-node service
   213	times INCLUDING kernel execution — not boundary costs.** A separate
   214	node-level trace of the same script yields, per chain segment (NVTX), the
   215	median kernel duration and the median serial inter-kernel gap; for non-PDL
   216	modes the serial gap is the boundary estimate; for PDL modes kernels overlap
   217	and no subtraction is attempted (pure boundary `UNRESOLVED`); the traced
   218	run's perturbation vs the unprofiled `T(N)` is reported. Prior calibration
   219	points to compare against: Hazy (H100 ≈ 2.1 µs stream, ≈ 1.3 µs graph);
   220	NVIDIA's CUDA 12.6 figure ≈ 0.55 µs/node is empty-kernel graph device
   221	service time (RTX 3060), not CPU launch cost. **Until measured, no
   222	per-boundary number is used anywhere**, and the ledger's X10 must not count
   223	kernel execution as removable boundary overhead. Any unavailable mode is
   224	recorded `UNTESTED(reason)`.
   225	
   226	## 5. Session structure, protocol, budget
   227	- **Protocol 5a**, reused from `arms/common.py::timed_run` (per-iteration
   228	  synchronize latency block of 50 passes inside the cudaProfilerApi range;
   229	  one free-running block of 50 passes outside it), **five repeats per
   230	  process**; per-process statistic = min-of-medians and median spread; per
   231	  (arm × chunk) headline = **median over 3 fresh-process restarts of the
   232	  per-process min-of-medians**, every per-iteration list preserved
   233	  (an explicit extension of `sm120` README deviation 5; spread = max − min of
   234	  the repeat medians; within-process and between-process variation are both
   235	  preserved). **Plan 1.2 matched comparisons** use `fullmodel/matched_run.py`:
   236	  two arms built, settled and validated in ONE fresh process, then their 5a
   237	  blocks ALTERNATE (a, b, a, b, …, five each) so every block pair is matched
   238	  in time and clocks; repeated in three fresh processes. Registered pairs at
   239	  352800: (C1, C3) the graph-break tax; (C1, C1D) the DC-filter repair
   240	  alone; (C3, H) the pad-and-batch delta; (C1, C1A) once, to record the
   241	  expected compile failure. The matrix restarts are additionally round-major
   242	  (r1 of every arm, then r2, then r3). Clocks logged in the background at
   243	  1 Hz with timestamp and GPU UUID (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=timestamp,uuid,
   244	  clocks.sm,clocks.mem,power.draw,temperature.gpu,utilization.gpu -l 1`) for
   245	  the whole session; timed blocks carry wall timestamps.
   246	- **Unprofiled timing is the primary result.** Session-2 traces are for
   247	  mechanism; profiled and unprofiled numbers never share a ratio. Profiling
   248	  perturbation (plan 1.6): A, C3, G at 4410 and 352800, wall unprofiled vs
   249	  `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`; >5 % inflation → every mechanism
   250	  number from that trace carries the factor as a caveat.
   251	- **Session 1 (≤ 3 h, RunPod secure-cloud RTX 5090, pod name
   252	  `melband_profiling`, image `runpod/pytorch:1.1.0-rc.154-cu1281-torch280-ubuntu2204`
   253	  = the pinned torch 2.8.0+cu128 / triton 3.4.0 matrix):** in this order —
   254	  setup (idempotent `setup.sh` + clip pack + nsys check); eager-A references
   255	  at both operating points; the two planted-fault checks; C3 operator
   256	  qualification per frame count; the matched pairs (§5, plan 1.2); the arm
   257	  matrix at 352800 and 4410 (three round-major restarts, SDR gate in r1 at
   258	  both points); C2 last. Budget guards (registered): a hard session deadline
   259	  (3 h) with a 15-min extraction reserve — a cell that cannot start before
   260	  the reserve is `UNTESTED(deadline)` and the session is INCOMPLETE, never
   261	  salvaged by weakening validation or timing; per-process caps (25 min
   262	  compile arms, 15 min others); an **aggregate C2 allowance of 40 min**, and
   263	  a timed-out cold C2 attempt is not retried at that operating point (the
   264	  remaining restarts are `UNTESTED`; a timeout means unavailable
   265	  performance, not evidence that C2 is slow). Every run's JSON carries
   266	  `headline_eligible` with machine-readable reasons; a headline per (arm ×
   267	  operating point) requires three complete eligible restarts with the same
   268	  effective variant (capture scope / adapter / compile mode), else the cell
   269	  is `INCOMPLETE`. Evidence extraction before terminate.
   270	- **Session 2 (≤ 3 h):** eager-A references + SDR at the sweep points; chunk
   271	  sweep (A, C3, G, H × {22050, 88200}, 3 restarts, SDR gate in r1);
   272	  node-level traces (ONE captured 50-pass block per traced run, outer NVTX
   273	  `pass_<i>` ranges, captured pass count stored; generated-code dump from
   274	  the SAME process for compiled arms) of A, A-NVTX, C3, G, H **and C1, B, E,
   275	  F** (graph verification §3; multi-stream timing views, plan 1.7) at both
   276	  operating points; profiling-perturbation runs (A, C3, G: one matching
   277	  block each, unprofiled vs `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`);
   278	  attention-backend microbench (plan 1.5: time-axis batch 60 × seq T and
   279	  freq-axis batch T × seq 60, heads 8, dim_head 64, fp16, contiguous
   280	  (b,h,n,d) inputs, flash / efficient / cuDNN / math forced one at a time,
   281	  CUDA-graph-replayed device timing, unsupported cases recorded; the model's
   282	  actual backend read from its trace); boundary microbench (§4) plus its
   283	  node-level trace; exports; evidence extraction. Same deadline/reserve rules.
   284	- Engineering shakedown of the harness on ml-beast GPU 4 is permitted before
